{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghnqA9joSV75MHQwRHMz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dq = 1750 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 618 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 2494 cm⁻¹ (C/B ≈ 4.0). The first two come in closed form from the three spin-allowed bands. C then comes from matching the sharp ²E_g band using a full diagonalisation of the d³ matrices.\n\n**Band assignment (octahedral d³, ground state ⁴A₂g)**\n- ν₁ = 17 500 cm⁻¹ is ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₂g.\n- ν₂ = 23 860 cm⁻¹ is ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₁g(F).\n- ν₃ = 37 910 cm⁻¹ is ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₁g(P).\n- The weak, sharp band at 12 090 cm⁻¹ is ⁴A₂g → ²E_g, the lowest spin-forbidden transition.\n\n**1. Dq**\nFor d³, ν₁ = 10Dq exactly, so 10Dq = 17 500 cm⁻¹ and **Dq = 1750 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**2. B**\nThe two ⁴T₁g energies satisfy ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B.\n- 23 860 + 37 910 = 61 770 = 52 500 + 15B\n- So **B ≈ 618 cm⁻¹**.\n\nCheck: E(⁴T₁g) = 15Dq + 7.5B ∓ ½√(225B² − 180·Dq·B + 100Dq²) gives 23 858 and 37 912 cm⁻¹. These match the observed bands to within 2 cm⁻¹.\n\n**3. C**\nThe ²E_g level depends on C, while the quartet levels do not. A quick estimate is E(²E) ≈ 9B + 3C − 90B²/(10Dq). This gives C ≈ 2830 cm⁻¹, which is too high.\n\nFull diagonalisation of the d³ matrices at Dq = 1750 and B = 618 gives:\n- C = 2830 → E(²E_g) = 13 113 cm⁻¹\n- C = 2480 → E(²E_g) = 12 046 cm⁻¹\n\nSo ²E_g rises by about 3.05 cm⁻¹ for every 1 cm⁻¹ increase in C. Interpolating to the observed 12 090 cm⁻¹ gives **C ≈ 2494 cm⁻¹**. I did not run a separate calculation at exactly C = 2494; the value comes from this interpolation.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1750, \"B\": 618, \"C\": 2494, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"10Dq comes from nu1, B from nu2+nu3, and C from fitting the 2Eg band by full d3 diagonalisation.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2192,"cache_read_input_tokens":10663,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2192,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens"